\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 The Suez crisis was the key turning point in the decline of the British empire from 1950-1968. Assess the validity of this view.\par
\par
In 1956 Colonel Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal company triggering an infamously botched Anglo-French reclamation operation assisted by the Israelis, resulting in international humiliation and condemnation. Since then historians have debated the significance of the Suez crisis in the decline of the British empire from 1950-1968. Undoubtedly it was important as it revealed to the public longer- term themes, such as economic decline, changing international relations and public attitudes. However to be considered a turning point the Suez crisis must represent a point in time at which an important change took place which affected the future decline of the British empire. This is not the case as the Suez crisis can largely be seen as an isolated incident symptomatic of wider underlying themes, rather than an important change in colonial policy leading to the decline of empire.\par
\par
However, there are several ways in which the Suez crisis can be considered a turning point in the decline of the British empire from 1950-1968. Firstly, the Suez crisis exposed the reality of Britain\rquote s world standing in the post war era. The submission of British foreign policy interests to the desires of Washington demonstrated that Britain had lost the capacity to act independently from the USA and reinforced the perception of Britain as the junior partner in the special relationship. It also  demonstrated the weakness and dependency of the sterling by 1956; whilst reaffirming the changing international attitudes as seen by Britain\rquote s portrayal as \ldblquote public enemy number one\rdblquote  within the United nations, of which Britain was supposed by an integral member, supporting and upholding international law. This was critical to the decline of empire as it demonstrated that Britain\rquote s lacked the resources \f1\endash  of financial muscle, military power, and geopolitical leverage \endash  to sustain an empire of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 such a vast scale and thus decolonisation was suddenly seen as both necessary and imminent.\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 Additionally, the serious damage inflicted on Britain\rquote s international relations with the U.S.A and the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 commonwealth led to an increased rate of decolonisation as a demonstration of good faith and a desire\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 to once again be seen as a progressive nation. Thus, within international relations the Suez marked a\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 key turning point, rapidly accelerating the rate of the decline of the British empire.\par
\par
Secondly in the metropole the Suez crisis made it brutally clear the British government had lost touch\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 with the ideological climate. Their claim to be the champions of freedom and progress had been\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 exposed as fraud on a monumental scale shattering the automatic public trust and confidence in the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 good faith of their government, who were now painted as self-interested. This was a key turning point\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 as it left a significant legacy contributing to decolonisation from 1950-1968. Immediately it sparked a\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 generation of protest with criticism of unethical foreign and imperial policy at the very front. Never\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 again would politicians receive the benefit of the doubt. Indeed Macmillan, a pragmatist, was left with\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 little choice other than to rapidly accelerate the process of decolonisation, as seen by the appointment\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 of progressive Colonial secretary Ian Macleod in 1959. Thus in terms of public attitudes the Suez\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 crisis can be seen as a key turning point leading to the decline of empire as it marked the end of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 deference to hierarchy and the beginning of a more progressive age, to which the government was\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 forced to react.\par
\par
Finally, for nationalists it demonstrated the weakness of post war colonial rule. Nasser\rquote s successful\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 seizure of the Suez canal and the mutilated and ineffective British response demonstrated to\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 nationalists throughout the colonies that Britain no longer possessed the means nor the will to retain\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 its empire by force; a result of both changing economic and social values as well as shifting\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 geopolitical powers (as discussed above). This undoubtedly increased the confidence of nationalist\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 movements and violent protests subsequently emerged across African colonies as seen in the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 Nyasaland emergency in 1959. Therefore it could be argued that the Suez crisis was the key turning\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 point in the decline of the British empire from 1950-1968 as it demonstrated the \ldblquote toothlessness of the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 British lion\rdblquote , not just to nationalists, but to the world, whilst simultaneously demonstrating the extent\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 to which imperialists had lost touch with attitudes within the metropole.\par
\par
However, when seeking to explain the decline of the British empire between 1950-1968 it can be\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 unhelpful and arguably incorrect to overemphasise the importance of Suez as a turning point. Indeed,\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 it is more plausible that the Suez crisis was symptomatic of wider trends which spelt the end of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 empire. Selwyn Lloyd, previously minister of defence and foreign secretary, took the view that 'Suez\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 became an excuse. It was the scapegoat for what was happening to Britain in the world, and for all\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 that flowed from the loss of power and economic weakness. It was in no way a cause of that loss of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 power or that weakness\rdblquote . Indeed, Eden himself wrote immediately after the crisis that the experience\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 had 'not so much changed our fortunes as revealed realities\rdblquote . Thus, it could be said that Suez was in\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 no way a turning point and that the roots of the decline of empire are found in longer term trends.\par
\par
One such trend was Britain\rquote s declining economic power. Independently of the Suez crisis\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 decolonisation was probable, if not inevitable, as maintenance of empire was no longer viable within\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 the post war British economy. By 1950, as Cooper has argued, \lquote Britain was adding up the costs and\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 benefits of colonial rule more carefully than ever before and coming up with negative numbers\rquote . This\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 is largely unsurprising given the limited economic value of many of the colonies and the growing cost\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 of repressing nationalist movements. Indeed, such costs were further exacerbated by the scarcity of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 resources post WW2 and the growing financial burdens of the welfare state and the nuclear deterrent\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 in the metropole, which were increasingly seen as fiscal priorities. Moreover, the economic worth of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 colonies was further damaged by declining trade: in 1948 46.1% of British exports went to\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 commonwealth countries yet by 1965 they made up just 27.9% as British exporters looked to markets\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 with higher incomes for manufactured goods and services. This decline in trade when combined with\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 colonies increasing financial burden for modernisation and growing costs within the metropole meant\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 that Britain simply no longer possessed the financial ability to retain such an expansive empire. Thus,\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 the British once again began to view colonies as \ldblquote millstones around their neck\rdblquote  (Disraeli) \endash  an\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 outlook which made decolonisation far more likely than any short-term consequence of the Suez\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 crisis.\par
\par
Another key trend in the decline of the British empire from 1950-1968 was changing international\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 relations, especially between Britain and America. The Suez crisis is frequently portrayed as a turning\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 point in international relations as it demonstrated that Britain was no longer a hegemonic power and\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 was reliant upon the U.S.A for international action. However, this is anachronistic. Indeed, far from\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 being a watershed moment the Suez crisis simple reaffirmed American supremacy. A fact that those\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 in Whitehall and Washington had known since 1945. Indeed prior to the Suez crisis, the dominance of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 American interests in the middle east had been made clear through large U.S loans to Saudi Arabi,\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 extensive support for the state of Israel and the instalment of the Shah in Iran. Additionally, to clas\f0\lang2057 s \f1\lang9 the Suez crisis as the turning point for decolonisation, with regards to international relations, would be\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 to ignore the importance of the Cold war which long predates 1956. During this period the threat of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 soviet expansion became an increasingly prevalent and predominant concern. As such the British\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 became more willing to see nationalists in colonial settings as allies against communism as well as\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 increasingly enfranchising colonial peoples as a buttress to potential nationalist-soviet alliances. A\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 process increasingly advocated for by a dominant United States in the aftermath of the Korean war,\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 and largely independent of the Suez crisis. Therefore, to the extent to which international relations\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 played a significant role in decolonisation the Suez crisis was not the turning point or cause but rather\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 an effect symptomatic of a shifting geopolitical landscape which had been in flux since the end of the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 second world war.\par
\par
Finally, one of the most significant reasons for decolonisation was changing public opinions in the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 metropole and increasing hostility towards the establishment and imperialism. Undoubtedly Suez was\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 significant in this regard as it struck a deep blow in public trust in the government however to\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 describe it as a turning point would once again be inaccurate, as it would be to confuse cause and\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 effect. Suez was a rallying point for a generation of \ldblquote Angry young men\rdblquote  in the post war era but it was\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 not the cause, it simply epitomised what was wrong with post war Britain and provided a licence to\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 rail against the arrogance and ineptitude of Britain\rquote s ruling class. Ultimately this change in attitudes\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 occurred on a much greater generational level and was the culmination of the end of an era of\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 deference to the establishment and the growing moral repugnance that some Britons felt towards the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 empire. This is unsurprising as the concepts of racial superiority which underlay imperialism,\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 following the struggle against Nazism and eugenics, had become deeply unfashionable in the post war\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 period. These changing attitudes were critical in the decline of the British empire from 1950-1968 as\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 they put increasing pressure on the government of the day, both Macmillan\rquote s and Wilson\rquote s, to\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 accelerate the process of decolonisation, especially within Africa. Therefore, it is clear that whilst\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 changing attitudes were important to the decline of the British Empire, they did not begin with the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 Suez crisis; rather it was simply the latest in a series of expressions of distaste towards the\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 establishment and thus not a turning point.\par
\par
In conclusion, it is clear that whilst the Suez crisis was a significant event in the decline of the British\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 empire it was did not constitute a turning point. This is because whilst it exposed to the public eye\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 some of key trends which would ultimately lead to the decline of empire; namely changing public\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 attitudes, international relations and economic weakness, it did not cause these trends and nor did it\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 significantly alter their trajectory, though it may have accelerated their progress. Thus, the view\f0\lang2057  \f1\lang9 expressed in the question statement is invalid.\f0\par
